 ],
 "literal": "Resembling the spotted face of a battle-fought elephant, / upon the very top of the large rock in the common ground, many together / bright red glory lilies bloom in the land of the lord. / Whether he is a virtuous man or not, / are we indeed his wall? Do they not have their own home, I wonder, / the people of this small hamlet who stay painfully, / where the pure white waterfall descending from the mountain / flows down by the useless huts?",
 "translation": "In the lord's land, many bright red glory lilies bloom together on the very top of the large rock in the common ground, resembling the spotted face of an elephant that has fought in battle. Whether he is a man of virtue or not, are we his only support? Do they not have a home of their own, I wonder—the people of this small hamlet who live in distress, where the pure white waterfall descending from the mountain flows past their useless huts?",
 "modern": "போரிட்ட யானையின் புள்ளிகள் நிறைந்த முகத்தைப் போல, பொதுமன்றத்தில் உள்ள பெரிய பாறையின் மேல் பலவாகச் சேர்ந்து பிரகாசமான செங்காந்தள் மலர்கள் மலரும் நாட்டை உடையவன் தலைவன். அவன் அறநெறி உடையவனோ அல்லது இல்லாதவனோ, நாம் மட்டும்தான் அவனுக்குப் பற்றுக்கோடா? மலைமேலிருந்து வீழ்ந்து, வீணான குடிசைகளின் வழியே பாயும் தூய வெள்ளருவி கொண்ட இந்தச் சிறிய குடியில் துன்பத்துடன் வாழும் மக்களுக்குத் சொந்தமாக வீடு இல்லையோ?",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the heroine's confidante (tōḻi) speaking to the heroine in a tone of frustration about the hero's delay in formalizing their marriage. She describes the hero's mountain land (Kuriñci), where bright red glory lilies bloom on a large rock, looking like the scarred, spotted face of a battle-hardened elephant. The confidante questions whether the hero is truly virtuous, as he leaves them in anxiety while the villagers of the small hamlet suffer from the loud, rushing waterfall flowing past their humble huts. The image of the glory lily blooming on the rock represents the heroine's love which is visible to everyone, yet the hero remains unmoving. The \"useless huts\" (koṉṉilaik kurampai) and the \"painful stay\" (iṉṉātu irunta) reflect the distress of the heroine and her companions due to the hero's prolonged absence and the gossip of the village.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
